<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jai Singh II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1688">1688</a>–<a href="/years/1743">1743</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sawai Jai Singh II, Sawai Jai Singh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jai Singh II was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwaha</a> raja of Amber in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> from 1699 to 1743 and a leading noble of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. In 1727 he founded the new capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, laid out on a grid plan. A keen astronomer, he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jantar-mantar">Jantar Mantar</a> observatories at Jaipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and had astronomical tables compiled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii.jpg" alt="Jai Singh II" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_Maharaja_Sawai_Jai_Singh_II_ca_1725_Jaipur._British_museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jai Singh II was born on 3 November 1688 and became the 30th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwaha</a> ruler of the Kingdom of Amber at the age of 11, after the death of his father, Mirza Raja Bishan Singh, on 31 December 1699. He inherited barely enough resources to support 1,000 cavalry. Because Amber lay close to the Mughal centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, its rulers had long preferred diplomacy to arms, and Jai Singh began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the empire. Ordered by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> wars, he received there the title Sawai, meaning one and a quarter, that is, more capable than one man.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Emperor Bahadur Shah I declared Jai Singh's younger brother, Vijay Singh, Raja of Amber and reduced Jai Singh to an ordinary jagirdar. Jai Singh answered with a political alliance with the Kingdom of Marwar and a marriage alliance with the Kingdom of Mewar, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> rebellion and the recovery of the lost territories. Three times governor (subahdar) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> between 1714 and 1737, he urged Emperor Muhammad Shah to come to terms with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and was removed; under his successor the whole of Malwa was ceded to them by the Treaty of Duraha in 1738.</p><p>Jai Singh enlarged his kingdom by purchase and by war, above all by acquiring Shekhawati, and armed his foot soldiers with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a> instead of sword and shield. His attempt to build a union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>'s lead, begun with a conference of rulers at Hurda in 1734, failed after the Battle of Gangwana. He was cremated at Gaitore, north of Jaipur, and succeeded by his son Ishwari Singh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Jai Singh performed ancient Vedic rites such as the Ashvamedha sacrifice, promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning, and pressed reforms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> society, including the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a>. At his insistence Muhammad Shah abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax on Hindus in 1720, and in 1728 withdrew the pilgrimage tax at Gaya. A dispute over astronomical calculations at the Mughal court in 1719 convinced him that astronomy needed to be taught, an interest perhaps kindled by his tutor Jagannatha Samrat. He had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements</a>, works on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a> and John Napier's work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logarithm">logarithms</a> translated into Sanskrit.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, first called Jai Nagara, was designed by Vidyadhar Bhattacharya on an ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> grid pattern and protected by thick walls and a garrison of 17,000. The observatories at Jaipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> still work; the one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> does not, and the one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> disappeared long ago.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sawai Jai Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sawai Jai Singh II</b> founded Jaipur in 1727 on a grid plan and built astronomical observatories, the Jantar Mantar, at Jaipur, Delhi and other cities.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaipur-state">Jaipur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Amber, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.98,75.87&amp;year=1723" aria-label="Amber on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jai Singh II"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3934 5192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3934 5192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.98,75.87&amp;year=1723">What was here in 1723? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
